# Each phrase points at a passage by where it sits (`described above`,
# `the next section`), so it breaks when someone moves the passage or
# adds one between. The fix links the section by name.
#
# The rule counts references to a passage, not to a block beside the
# sentence. `the example below` and `the table above` usually sit next
# to their block, the way `the following` does, so they stay out. The
# required back-reference rule reports `as described above`, so the
# filter skips a verb after `as`. Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter
# consumes the word before and narrows the finding back to the phrase.
#
# A bare `the above` stays out too, because it usually points at the
# code block right above it (`The above produces the same output`). It
# counts before a noun that names a passage (`the above scenario`, `the
# preceding risks`) and as the object of a word that takes the whole
# passage (`given the above`, `in addition to the above`).
#
# It hit 70 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled hits
# pointed at a passage by position.
expr where [above, below]
expr verbs [see, described, mentioned, explained, discussed, outlined]
expr passage [situation, situations, scenario, scenarios, problem, problems, (use cases), tradeoffs, limitations, risks, considerations]
expr main [<(!as @verbs @where), (@verbs @where)>, (the [section, sections, paragraph, chapter] @where), (the [next, previous, preceding] [section, chapter]), (the [above, preceding] @passage), ([given, following, notwithstanding, despite, besides, (in addition to), (based on)] the above)]
let message "Positional reference. Link the section by name."
let description "Flags described above, the next section, and similar references that point at a passage by position."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The backoff follows the restart policy in Pod Lifecycle."
allows "The header is parsed as described above."
allows "In the example below, the claim requests one device."
allows "The above produces the same output."
